<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lech (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lech is a river of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 255 kilometres long, flowing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>. For much of its course it formed the boundary between Bavarian and Swabian lands. The Lechfeld, the plain south of Augsburg, was the site of Otto I's victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a> in 955.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lech-river.jpg" alt="Lech (river)" width="320" height="685" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Sansculotte at German Wikipedia. Later versions were uploaded by 217.85.228.77, Paddy at de... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karte_einzugsbereich_lech.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
